le for what's about to happen  for the next hour  i don't have to have a lesson plan that  could go well or disastrously  i definitely early on took a while to  find my feet my  sort of persona as a teacher you had to  learn all these skills that didn't come  naturally like  how to be angry i mean anger comes  naturally to some teachers but didn't  come naturally to me well  in my day young man it used to be the  teacher would simply be angry by pinging  a little bit of chalk at your head  that's right i've had a gut full of you  fired look  great that was a very good first  impression i can tell you in the head  with a piece of chalk  i i still remember a woman who i met  out in the riverina she must've been 75  or 80 years old and she said when  we got asked to do a maths problem we  would be instructed to write the answer  to that problem on our hand  then the teacher after a few minutes say  hold up your answers so i can see them  on your hands  and then that teacher would go to the  front of the room she would open the  drawer  out of her desk and she would pull out  her cane  and any student who had the wrong answer  written on their hands they never forgot  for the rest of their lives as this  woman didn't  thankfully we don't do that anymore no  that's a bad thing that's a bad thing  but uh you have to have other ways to  earn the respect of you especially in  high school  how do you how do you do that do you do  you simmer do you look do you do you  raise your voice or do you go quiet  you've got to have the whole set of  tools in your toolbox  um one of the things which i realized as  a teacher you know you're trying to  explain an idea a concept a skill and it  doesn't cut it  to know it well enough to answer those  questions in the textbook and then you  go into the room and then you just tell  the kids  what you know one way is not enough you  need  15 different ways to approach an idea  and it's the same with  classroom student discipline knowing  what