et people in a  certain career you pay them more and the  answer is  yes to a certain point when people are  engaged in a creative  pursuit which and teaching is a very  creative industry  it's it's not that you pay the more you  get better results you pay them until  the point that they don't need to worry  about their income and their money and  then once they don't have to worry about  it they can  focus on their job they can think i'm  not getting second best because i'm  choosing this career you know i live in  sydney  on a teacher's salary alone if it's a  single income family you can afford a  pretty decent cardboard box but it is a  cardboard box nonetheless  and you've got lots of teachers who out  of their own pockets are buying things  for their own classes for their students  i every year we'll have  you know a bunch of kids in our school  who can't afford a calculator which they  need it's essential not that it's that  expensive but they don't have the  disposable income for it but teachers  are putting their hands in their own  pockets to pay for this  all the time all the time and so what  you want is to say  for schools and for individual teachers  that we have the resources to allow you  to  in an unfettered way concentrate on  doing your job best and caring for these  kids is it worse in rural areas  in rural areas the main challenge is  that it's hard to have teachers out  there  who have the right qualifications and  who'll stay um it's fairly common for  teachers to go out there for maybe  uh two or three years but then they  leave and and that's kind of  fatal you know because teaching so much  about relationships  you really it takes those three four  five years to become part of the  community know the kids their parents  and that investment is difficult to ask  of someone who's come from a city or  metro area suburban area  and to move out there relocated their  life so those areas are really difficult  to start and they're doing it tough  y